<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Timor is an island at the southern end of the Malay Archipelago, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, divided today between the independent state of Timor-Leste in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a>. Its sandalwood drew Chinese, Malay and Portuguese traders, and from the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> held the west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a> while the Portuguese held the east. Many small kingdoms on the island acknowledged the ritual seniority of Wehali.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timor.jpg" alt="Timor" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timor_island2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Timor lies in the north of the Timor Sea, at the eastern end of the Lesser Sunda Islands, and its name is a variant of timur, Malay for east. Mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> is less than 500 km away. The island covers 30,777 square kilometres, and its mountains reach nearly 3,000 metres. Mutis is the highest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a> and Ramelau the highest in Timor-Leste.</p><p>The climate has a long dry season from April to November, with hot winds blowing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Anthropologists identify eleven ethno-linguistic groups on the island, the largest being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atoni">Atoni</a> of the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetum-language">Tetun</a> of the centre and east. Most indigenous languages belong to the Timor-Babar branch of the Austronesian family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest historical record of Timor is the Chinese Zhu Fan Zhi of the 13th century, which calls it Ti-wu and notes its sandalwood. The Javanese Nagarakretagama of the 14th century names it as an island within the realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. By then Timor was part of Javanese, Chinese and Indian trading networks, exporting sandalwood, slaves, honey and wax.</p><p>The Portuguese and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> later settled there, the Dutch from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a> in the mid-17th century, and they fought for control until a treaty of 1859 divided it, though the boundary was not formally settled until 1912. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a> was Dutch Timor until 1949, when it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> was a Portuguese colony until 1975.</p><p>In 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a> and the seamen loyal to him reached Timor after the mutiny on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">Bounty</a>. In December 1941 Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces occupied Portuguese Timor, and after the Japanese landed in February 1942 a 400-strong Dutch-Australian force and many Timorese volunteers fought a year-long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign. The Japanese occupation killed between 40,000 and 70,000 Timorese.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974 the Portuguese withdrew, and in 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> invaded and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a>, an annexation the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and Portugal never recognised. Falintil resisted in a prolonged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until a UN-sponsored referendum in 1999 rejected autonomy within Indonesia. The UN governed the territory until it became independent as Timor-Leste in 2002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is divided between Timor-Leste in the east, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a>, part of the Indonesian province of East Nusa Tenggara, whose provincial capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a>. Timor-Leste also holds the exclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecusse">Oecusse</a> within West Timor. The official languages of Timor-Leste are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetum-language">Tetun</a> and Portuguese, and in West Timor Indonesian. Christianity is the dominant religion across the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Timor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timor-Leste's name means 'East East': <b>Timor</b> comes from timur, 'east' in Indonesian or Malay, and Leste is 'east' in Portuguese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>They came when Australia was part of Sahul, joined to New Guinea by land, but still had to cross the sea beyond the Wallace Line, perhaps island-hopping from Sulawesi or by way of <b>Timor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Bligh navigated some 6,700 km to <b>Timor</b> with the loss of only one man.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dominican missionaries and traders from the Portuguese base on Solor had been active on <b>Timor</b> since the 16th century, buying sandalwood.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-timor">Portuguese Timor</a></li><li><blockquote>Wewiku-Wehali lay on the south coast of central <b>Timor</b>, in today's Malaka Regency.</blockquote><a href="/state/wehali">Wehali</a></li></ul></section>
